<p><strong>Beauty parlour software for Nepal has to cope with something a general booking tool does not expect: services that run from five minutes to five hours, booked against a named beautician, in a parlour where half the clients walk in. Get the durations and the person right and the rest of the day holds together. If you are comparing products rather than learning how this works, read the <a href="/blog/best-salon-software-nepal/">salon and parlour software buyer's guide</a>.</strong></p>

<p>In Nepal, &ldquo;beauty parlour&rdquo; is the everyday name for what other markets call a salon: hair, facials, threading, waxing, nails, bridal makeup and often a small spa corner under one roof. That breadth is the whole scheduling problem. A hair salon books hour-long slots all day. A parlour books a 5-minute eyebrow threading, a 90-minute facial and a 4-hour bridal session into the same beautician's afternoon, and three cousins arrive unannounced while it is happening.</p>

<h2>The parlour service mix, and why fixed slots fail</h2>
<p>Most registers and simple diaries work in fixed blocks &mdash; half an hour, an hour. In a parlour that quietly throws away a third of the day. Threading, upper-lip and eyebrow work take minutes; a bridal trial takes an afternoon. If every service occupies a 30-minute block, the short ones waste 25 minutes each and the long ones overrun into the next client.</p>
<p>What works instead: every service carries its own real duration, including the setup and cleanup it genuinely needs, and bookings sit against a beautician rather than a generic slot. Short services then fill the real gaps between long ones. See <a href="/features/booking/">booking management</a> for how the calendar handles this.</p>

<h2>Booking by beautician, not by chair</h2>
<p>Parlour clients ask for a person. They have a beautician who does their threading the way they like it, and they will wait rather than take someone else. That makes the beautician the thing being booked, which has two consequences worth planning for.</p>
<p>First, a slot has to be held against that person so two clients are never promised the same beautician &mdash; the mistake that loses a regular rather than a slot. Second, when that beautician is absent or moves to another branch for the day, every booking she holds needs to surface rather than be discovered at the door. <a href="/blog/therapist-absent-branch-short-staffed-nepal/">What to do when a staff member is absent</a> covers the recovery.</p>

<h2>Walk-ins alongside booked clients</h2>
<p>A parlour cannot turn away a walk-in, and should not make a booked client wait for one. The workable arrangement is a queue that sits beside the calendar: walk-ins are added against whichever beautician is genuinely free, booked appointments keep their slot, and the front desk can see an honest wait time instead of guessing. The approach is the same one <a href="/blog/walk-in-queue-management-salon-barbershop-nepal/">salons and barbershops use for walk-in queues</a>.</p>

<h2>Bridal and festival bookings, booked months out</h2>
<p>Bridal is the parlour's highest-value work and the most fragile: one date, several hours, often a trial weeks earlier, and a client who booked in Asar for a Mangsir wedding. Those bookings need a deposit recorded against them so the date is genuinely committed, and they need to survive staff changes in between. <a href="/blog/bridal-makeup-artist-booking-nepal/">Bridal makeup booking in Nepal</a> goes into the deposit and wedding-season side.</p>
<p>The same pressure arrives twice a year regardless of weddings. During Dashain and Tihar a parlour can take a month's bookings in a fortnight &mdash; see <a href="/blog/dashain-tihar-salon-spa-bookings-nepal/">how to prepare the schedule for the festival rush</a>.</p>

<h2>Packages, prepaid cards and what is left on them</h2>
<p>Parlours sell in advance more than most service businesses: six-facial courses, bridal packages, prepaid cards bought as gifts. The recurring failure is nobody knowing what remains on one, so sessions get given away twice or refused wrongly. A balance that decrements on redemption, visible to whoever is at the desk, is the fix. See <a href="/features/packages/">packages</a>, <a href="/features/memberships/">memberships</a> and <a href="/features/vouchers/">vouchers</a>, and <a href="/blog/gift-voucher-package-balance-tracking-spa-nepal/">how to keep those balances honest</a>.</p>

<h2>Commission, because beauticians are paid on it</h2>
<p>Beauticians in Nepal are typically paid a salary plus commission on the services they deliver. That only works if the system knows who delivered what, which it does automatically once bookings sit against a named person. <a href="/features/payroll/">Payroll</a> then works out salary, absence deductions and commission from the bookings themselves, instead of the owner reconstructing it from a register at month end.</p>

<h2>Where to start</h2>
<p>Enter your real services with their real durations first &mdash; that single step is what makes the rest useful &mdash; then turn on online booking and payment recording, and add discount approval, packages and reports as the team settles in. Most front-desk staff are comfortable within a day. See how Zennly works for <a href="/solutions/salon/">salons and beauty parlours</a>, <a href="/solutions/bridal-makeup/">bridal and makeup studios</a> and <a href="/solutions/beauty-clinic/">beauty clinics</a>, or <a href="#try-free" data-trial-open>try the free demo</a> with your own service list.</p>
